Question: is POST /v1/embeddings distributed over two nodes of the same model today, and is its usage counted?

Checked against main (2b0c049), two fake nodes serving one model, max_conns 2 each:
- routing: yes. /v1/embeddings goes through _begin_route_async like chat: route.plan (policy, alias tiers), route.state, route.next, request.start. Six sequential requests land 3/3.
- health: a node set unhealthy gets nothing, the other takes all.
- admission: six concurrent slow requests -> four admitted (peak 2 per node), two answered 429 after route_wait; inflight is back to 0 afterwards.
- capacity probes: the same _capacity_allows as chat; a vLLM embedding node is covered by the Prometheus probe defaults.
- usage: one event per forwarded request, endpoint /v1/embeddings; usage.prompt_tokens / total_tokens become input_tokens / total_tokens, priced by the model's input_per_million. output_tokens is 0.
- client_auth (k90) already gates the route (t/67).

What is missing:
1. No test pins any of this: the suite calls /v1/embeddings with one node only (t/38, t/61, t/67). Build: a test with two embedding nodes -- distribution, health, max_conns/429, alias tier, tokens and cost in the usage event, TEI- and infinity-shaped answers.
2. Docs do not say that an embedding server is just a node: README node examples (vLLM, TEI, infinity), with the caveats below.
3. Caveats to document, not to build:
   - infinity serves /embeddings at the root unless started with --url-prefix /v1, and its usage counts characters unless lengths_via_tokenize is on
   - the Prometheus probe's default metric names cover vLLM, SGLang and TGI; a TEI or infinity node names its own metrics in the capacity block, or runs on inflight admission
   - one request is one slot whatever its batch size: a 1000-input request weighs like a 1-input one. max_conns has to be chosen with that in mind
   - a JSON body above Mojolicious' 16 MiB message limit is refused as invalid JSON
   - there is no Ollama embeddings face (/api/embed)

Done when: the test exists and is green, README and the skeid-protocols skill show an embedding node, and whatever the test turns up as a defect is fixed or filed as its own card.

